{\pard\sb360\sa120\keepn\b Interest criteria \u183? version 0.1\par}
{\pard\sa160 Published object 5 (appendix D.1). The criteria by which this community judges whether what is new in a paper is of interest. The editor and two named reviewers sign this judgment; it is the one judgment no machine input decides. Demonstration text: each journal writes its own, in its own words, and this is where journals differ.\par}
{\pard\sa160 A paper that a signed editor has judged new is of interest to this community when at least one holds:\par}
{\pard\li360\fi-360\sa60 1.\tab {\b It changes what a reader in the field would predict or do.} A result that reverses, bounds or sharpens an expectation the field acts on.\par}
{\pard\li360\fi-360\sa60 2.\tab {\b It connects streams that do not read each other.} A result that a reader in one stream needs and would not otherwise find.\par}
{\pard\li360\fi-360\sa60 3.\tab {\b It makes a question answerable.} A method, construct or formal device that lets others ask something they could not ask before.\par}
{\pard\li360\fi-360\sa60 4.\tab {\b It settles a live disagreement.} A result that bears directly on a question the community is divided on.\par}
{\pard\sa160 Reviewers judge interest only. They are not asked whether the paper is correct, well formed or new: the machine's findings, the editor's intake decision and the editor's "new" signature have answered those. A judgment of "not of interest" produces no public object.\par}
